# ── Chapter 6 fidelity pins: "Clawback" ──────────────────────────────────────
#
# KIND: CONTENT pin. Run:
#   ./verify.sh --only book-1/06-clawback.pins.nibli
#   ./verify.sh          # the real check — the line above cannot see
#                        # the cross-file reconciliation or the prose guards
#
# NOTE: the Cira pins below encode a defect the chapter names as indefensible.
# If the contamination rule is narrowed (claw back only recognition derived from
# the fraudulent teaching), lose(Points, Cira) flips FALSE and the chapter's
# middle section must be rewritten. That is the intended outcome, not a break.
#
# The reward(Cira) and person(Fin) pins are the chapter's sharpest claim and the
# two facts an earlier draft got backwards: the rule strips a student who never
# earned anything, and the student held up as keeping everything is not on the
# personhood roster at all. Being taught mints nothing — a settled decision, not
# an oversight — so the "narrower rule" the chapter discusses has an empty target
# and narrowing it is repeal. Keep these pinned; the prose turns on both.

:expect-pins 27

# "Bela was examined by two auditors and voided ... recognition is clawed back"
? false(Bela).
# => TRUE

? lose(Points, Bela).
# => TRUE

# "Bela is still a person. Bela still moves freely."
? person(Bela).
# => TRUE

? travel(Bela).
# => TRUE

? prisoner(Bela).
# => FALSE

# "...still holds the vote. And Bela is still owed everything on the floor."
? decide(Bela, Ballot).
# => TRUE

? entitled(Bela, event { eats() }).
# => TRUE

? owe(State, Eats, Bela).
# => TRUE

# THE PINS THAT WOULD HAVE CAUGHT THE OLD SENTENCE. The chapter used to say a
# voided person "still eats. Still has somewhere to live." The record says
# neither, about Bela or about anybody — being owed is not having, and the
# chapter that owns that gap is chapter 8. Jala is the control: never accused,
# never voided, and the same FALSE — which is what proves the verdict is the
# delivery gap and not the voiding. If eats(Bela) ever turns TRUE while
# eats(Jala) stays FALSE, something made the floor conditional on the finding,
# and both chapters are wrong.
? eats(Bela).
# => FALSE

? dwell(Bela).
# => FALSE

? eats(Jala).
# => FALSE

# "Bela taught Cira. Bela was voided. The rule reaches Cira too."
? teaches(Bela, Cira).
# => TRUE

# The chapter calls this indefensible outright: a student who earned nothing is
# stripped for a teacher's fraud. It flips FALSE when Article 4's contamination
# rule is narrowed or deleted — the intended outcome, not a break.
:defect "narrowing or deleting Article 4's contamination rule (teaches & false -> lose)"
? lose(Points, Cira).
# => TRUE

# "Cira earned nothing. Being taught is not a way to earn recognition here."
? reward(Cira).
# => FALSE

# "The rule reaches Cira's recognition and finds nothing there. It does not
#  reach what Cira is owed" — and it does not reach Cira's CREDIBILITY either.
# The loss lands on recognition alone; Cira is docked, not voided. If this ever
# flips TRUE the contamination rule grew a second head and the closing section
# is wrong about what the instrument can touch.
? false(Cira).
# => FALSE

# "Compare Fin, whose teacher was Esa. Esa was never voided, so the rule never
#  reaches Fin at all." — the rule fires only through the teacher
? teaches(Esa, Fin).
# => TRUE

# and Fin, the student held up as the lucky one, is not even a person here
? person(Fin).
# => FALSE

? false(Esa).
# => FALSE

? lose(Points, Fin).
# => FALSE

# the same clawback reaches the other voided auditor
? false(Lupo).
# => TRUE

? lose(Points, Lupo).
# => TRUE

# and Mira, accused by a liar, keeps everything
? lose(Points, Mira).
# => FALSE

# ── The epoch carry, in the chapter whose subject it serves ──────────────────
# Vex is the one person in the cast who is voided, stripped of the pen, still
# possessed of standing, and clawed back — and this file, the clawback
# chapter's own, never touched him. The carry is why: rotten is the mark
# written forward from a prior period, it re-derives the void THIS period, and
# that is the only reason the clawback keeps firing on a person whose voiding
# nobody in this epoch performed. Five verdicts, and between them they hold
# apart three things this book keeps having to hold apart: voided is not
# de-personed, losing the pen is not losing standing, and a prior period's
# judgment costs you here and now.
? rotten(Vex).
# => TRUE

? false(Vex).
# => TRUE

# the cost — pinned in no other file
? lose(Points, Vex).
# => TRUE

# the pen goes; the standing that makes you accountable does not
? permits(Review, Vex).
# => FALSE

? authority(Vex).
# => TRUE
